# Auto commit and push

After finishing a task that changes tracked files in this repo, **commit and push automatically**. Do not wait for the user to say "commit" or "push".

## When to commit and push

- Docs, scripts, rules, skills, or other tracked project files were added or edited as part of the completed task
- The work is in a good state (no half-finished edits left behind intentionally)

## When not to commit

- No tracked-file changes (only `workspace/` or other gitignored paths)
- The user explicitly said not to commit in this turn
- A commit hook failed — fix the issue and create a **new** commit (do not amend unless hook rules allow)
- Files likely contain secrets (`.env`, keys, credentials) — warn instead of committing

Never commit ISO images, `.bin` blobs, or other binaries under `workspace/` (gitignored).

## How to commit

1. `git status` and `git diff` to see what changed
2. Stage only relevant tracked files
3. Write a concise message (1–2 sentences, focus on why)
4. Commit — follow `.cursor/rules/no-cursor-commit-trailers.mdc` (no Cursor attribution)
5. `git push` to the current branch's remote (use `-u` only when the branch has no upstream yet)

Do not force-push. Do not skip hooks. Do not update git config.

## After push

Briefly tell the user what was committed and pushed (commit hash or message is enough). No need to ask permission first.
